Logic :

solved this problem using an iterative approach. 
start with the empty subset because every set always contains the empty subset. Then, It process each element of the input array one by one.
For every existing subset, create a new subset by copying it and adding the current element. 
The original subset remains unchanged, while the new subset includes the current element.
These newly formed subsets are added to the result list.
After processing all the elements, the result contains every possible subset of the given array.

Intuition : 

Make a copy of every subset and put me into the copy.


Code :

class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> subsets(int[] arr) {
        List<List<Integer>> outer = new ArrayList<>();
        outer.add(new ArrayList<>());
        for (int i=0;i<arr.length;i++) {
            int index=0;
            int n=outer.size();
            for (int j = index; j < n; j++) {
                List<Integer> inner = new ArrayList<>(outer.get(j));
                inner.add(arr[i]);
                outer.add(inner);
            }
        }
        return outer;
    }
}
